# Backend

## Stack

- Runtime: **Bun** · Framework: **Elysia** · DB: **MySQL** (Bun SQL)
- Use `Bun.env` (not `process.env`). Use ESM `import` only (no `require`).
- Commit / push only when explicitly requested.

## Layers

| Layer | Path | Role |
|-------|------|------|
| Routes | `routes/` | Elysia schema + wire handler only |
| Services | `services/` | Business logic / handlers |
| DB | `db/` | SQL only |
| Libs | `libs/` | Infra (DB pool, mailer, S3) |
| Middleware | `middlewares/` | Session / API auth |

- Do **not** put business logic or SQL in route files — `route` → `service` → `db`.
- Prefer schema-first when building features; implement UI only when asked.

## Database

- Schema lives only in `libs/db.js` → `generateDBTables()` (`CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`).
- **Do not** write migrations, `ALTER TABLE`, or backfill scripts for schema changes.
- After commit/push, the DB is dropped and recreated from scratch — assume a clean schema on next boot.
- Prefer generated columns when a value is always derived (e.g. `amount = cost * quantity`).
- Seed reference rows with `INSERT IGNORE` (e.g. `SERVICES`).
- DB helpers take a single object: `getX({ id })`, not positional args.
- When a table column is nullable, default that db param to `null` in the signature (e.g. `createThing({ col1, col2 = null })`). Do not add redundant `?? null` in the service when the db default already covers omission.
- On **INSERT**, omit columns that already have a schema `DEFAULT` unless the caller intentionally overrides that default. Do not restate `active = true`, `total = 0`, `active: false`, etc. in `create*` / services — leave them out of the INSERT column list so MySQL applies the default (`token`, `invoiced`, timestamps, etc. already follow this).

## Naming (ums_api style)

Paired names so service/db never collide:

| Intent | `services/` | `db/` |
|--------|-------------|-------|
| add | `addX` | `createX` |
| edit | `updateX` | `updateXById` / `updateXBy…` |
| delete | `deleteX` | `deleteXById` / `deleteXBy…` |
| read one | `getX` | `getXById` / `getXBy…` |
| read many | `getXs` | `getAllXs` |

Only **add** uses a different verb (`add` / `create`). Update / delete / get keep the same verb; db adds a qualifier (`ById`, `ByApplicationId`, `getAll…`).

### Local variables

Do **not** use bare names like `existing` or `updated`. Name the entity:

```js
// ❌ BAD
const existing = await getUserById({ id });
if (!existing) { … }

// ✅ GOOD
const existingUser = await getUserById({ id });
if (!existingUser) { … }

const updatedInvoice = await getCustomerInvoiceById({ id });
```

Same idea for lookups before create (`existingUser` by email) and post-update reloads (`updatedApplication`).

## Validation & helpers

- Elysia `t.` body / params / query schemas **must be inline** on each route. Never assign a shape to a top-level `const` (or helper) and reuse it on another route. Duplicate the schema on POST and PATCH even when fields match.
- Validate request shape **only** at the route (`minItems`, `minLength`, types, etc.). Do **not** re-check those in `services/` — services do existence/ownership/business rules only.
- Do **not** extract a helper for logic used in only one place — inline it. Extract only when the same logic is shared (two or more call sites). Exported route/service/db entry points and shared utils are fine even if a given file calls them once.

## Type casts

Skip redundant `Number` / `String` / `Boolean` / `typeof` when Elysia schemas or DB helpers already give the right type.

**Keep** casts that prevent real breaks:

- `Number(...)` on MySQL COUNT / SUM / DECIMAL aggregates (drivers may return strings)
- `Number(Bun.env.…)` for numeric env vars
- `String(n).padStart` / `` `${n}`.padStart `` when formatting OTP or date parts

```js
// ❌ BAD — body fields already typed by the route schema
const id = Number(body.id);

// ✅ GOOD — aggregate may be a string
.then((result) => Number(result?.[0]?.count ?? 0))
```

## Object spread (no manual rebuilds)

Do **not** copy an object property-by-property into a new literal. Spread, then override only what changes.

```js
// ❌ BAD — rebuilds x by hand
const y = { prop1: x.prop1, prop2: x.prop2 };

// ✅ GOOD
const y = { ...x };
const y = { ...x, prop2: override };
```

Same for passing args: prefer `{ ...body, title: body.title.trim() }` over listing every field from `body` again. Only rebuild field-by-field when you must **rename**, **omit**, or **reshape** (e.g. session DTO that must not leak `otp`).

## Routes & auth

- Admin UI under `/app/*` is session-guarded with `requireSession` — use `return redirect("/login")` (Elysia 1.4).
- Admin JSON APIs use `requireApiSession` and return `401` JSON (not redirect).
- Admin auth is email OTP via `/api/authentication-tokens` (POST create, PATCH verify). Cookie name: `authentication_token`, max-age **24 hours**. Guards must validate cookie token against `USER_AUTHENTICATION_TOKENS` where `active = TRUE`.
- Prefer **flat** resource paths — `/app/projects/:id`, `/app/invoices/:id`, `/app/applications/:id`, `/app/application-services/:id`. Avoid nested UI paths like `/app/customers/:id/projects/:projectId/...`.
- Same for APIs: `/api/customer-projects`, `/api/invoice-items`, `/api/application-services` — parent ids go in the body, not nested URL segments.
- Missing entities → redirect `/not-found` (UI) or `404` JSON (API).

## External service APIs — do not touch

These endpoints are consumed by **external client apps** via application token auth. **Never** change paths, request/response shapes, auth middleware, or related handlers unless the user explicitly asks.

| Method | Path |
|--------|------|
| `POST` | `/api/services/mailer` |
| `POST` | `/api/services/bucketizer` |
| `GET` | `/api/services/bucketizer/:file` |

Also leave alone the wiring and implementations behind them unless explicitly requested: `routes/apps/mailer.js`, `routes/apps/bucketizer.js`, `services/mailer.js`, `services/bucketizer.js`, `middlewares/parse_application.js`, `middlewares/can_use_mailer.js`, `middlewares/can_use_bucketizer.js`, and the `/api/services` group in `routes/api.js`.
